<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taif Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Document of National Accord · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanese-civil-war">Lebanese Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a> Agreement was negotiated by Lebanese members of parliament at Taif in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and signed in October 1989 to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanese-civil-war">Lebanese Civil War</a>. It shared parliamentary seats equally between Christians and Muslims, reduced the powers of the Maronite president in favour of the cabinet, and called for militias to disarm. It also recognised special ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, whose troops stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> until 2005.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The accord was fathered by the Speaker of Parliament, Hussein El-Husseini, and negotiated by the surviving members of the parliament elected in 1972, with active mediation by Saudi Arabia, discreet participation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> behind the scenes. Rafik Hariri, a former Saudi diplomatic representative, played a significant part in drafting it. It came amid a crisis of a split premiership: on 22 September 1988 President Amine Gemayel had appointed General Michel Aoun, a Maronite, as prime minister, a post reserved for a Sunni Muslim and held by Omar Karami.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The agreement made mutual coexistence between the sects the aim of future electoral laws and moved power away from the Maronite community, privileged since French rule. The Chamber of Deputies grew to 128 seats, the ratio of Christians to Muslims changed from 6:5 to 1:1, and the cabinet was divided the same way.</p><p>Executive power passed from the president to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> prime minister became responsible to parliament, and the Speaker's term was extended from one year to four. The abolition of political sectarianism was named a national priority without any timetable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a> was allowed to keep its arms as a resistance force in the south, a privilege the Swedish academic Magnus Ranstorp links in part to the Western hostages it held.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The parliament ratified the accord on 5 November 1989 at the Qoleiat air base and elected René Moawad president, but he was killed by a car bomb in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> on 22 November and succeeded by Elias Hrawi, whose signature in September 1990 brought the constitutional amendments into force. After Aoun fled to the French embassy during the Syrian assault on Baabda Palace on 13 October 1990, the army dismantled the Green Line in Beirut, and 30 April 1991 was set as the date for militias to disband. In July 1991 the army took the Palestinian positions around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> in three days of fighting. Syrian forces kept their hold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> until March 2005, after the Cedar Revolution and UN Resolution 1559.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taif Agreement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…128 seats, elected for four years by sectarian proportional representation, is divided equally between Christians and Muslims, a ratio of 6:5 before the <b>Taif Agreement</b> of 1989; it elects the president for a single six-year term by two-thirds, and the president appoints the prime minister after consulting it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
